// SLOVENIA — Smartious city-level + country-level data
// Live online Cambridge IGCSE, A-Level, Pearson Edexcel, IB Diploma,
// and American AP for expat, industrial, and Slovenian families
// across Ljubljana, Maribor, Koper, Kranj, Novo mesto and Nova Gorica.
// COMPLETES THE CENTRAL EUROPEAN CLUSTER (with Austria, Hungary,
// Czech Republic, Slovakia, Croatia's neighbours).
//
// LEGAL POSITIONING NOTE — READ BEFORE EDITING:
// Slovenia is a NOTIFICATION-RIGHT tier market with a STRICT
// ASSESSMENT EXIT. Permissive entry, demanding annual accounting.
// - The Basic School Act (Zakon o osnovni šoli, ZOsn) devotes a
//   whole Part (VII) to izobraževanje na domu and frames Art. 88 as
//   a RIGHT to education at home. Say "right", not "permission" —
//   this is genuinely different from Hungary, Austria, Slovakia,
//   and Luxembourg.
// - ENTRY IS BY WRITTEN NOTIFICATION to the osnovna šola where the
//   child is enrolled, for EACH school year separately. Parents do
//   NOT have to justify their reasons, and the school issues NO
//   administrative act — no permission, no consent. The notification
//   states the child's name, the place where the education will
//   take place, and the name(s) of the person(s) who will teach.
//   TIMING: historically "by the start of the school year"; the
//   2024 amendment moved the deadline earlier, to 16 August. State
//   it as "by mid-August (16 August under the amended Act)" and
//   tell families to confirm the current date with their school.
//   Home education CANNOT be started mid-year; the amendment does
//   allow interrupting it mid-year to return to school.
// - THE STANDARD: the pupil must attain AT LEAST AN EQUIVALENT
//   EDUCATIONAL STANDARD to that of the compulsory public-school
//   programme.
// - THE EXIT IS STRICT AND MUST ALWAYS BE STATED: knowledge is
//   assessed AT THE END OF EVERY SCHOOL YEAR by the school where
//   the child is enrolled, and the reform equalising home-educated
//   pupils with school pupils widened the range of subjects
//   assessed. CONSEQUENCE OF FAILURE: a pupil in grades 1-8 who
//   does not pass the re-assessment MUST continue their basic
//   education in a public or private school the following year;
//   a grade 9 pupil may repeat the year or sit a resit. Home-
//   educated pupils also take the national assessment (NPZ).
//   The school issues the spričevalo — a public document — noting
//   home education under remarks.
// - Assessment runs against the Slovenian national curriculum, in
//   Slovene: plan Slovene-language preparation all year in any
//   dual-track configuration (the Hungary/Slovakia discipline).
// - CONSEQUENCE FOR US: the assessment is the family's obligation
//   with their enrolling school, and it is unforgiving. We are the
//   academic spine — live teaching, documentation, external
//   examinations — and we NEVER imply we can carry or waive the
//   annual assessment. Supplementary beside an ordinary enrolment
//   remains the simplest configuration for most expat families.
// - THE WINDOW: osnovna šola is nine years, ages ~6-15. Upper
//   secondary (gimnazija or vocational) is NOT compulsory, so the
//   A-Level years fall outside the framework — including outside
//   the annual assessment. Use it prominently.
// MARKET NOTE: the international tier is a Ljubljana story — the
// British International School of Ljubljana, QSI, and the
// long-established international department at Danila Kumar (a
// public school running an international programme). Maribor and
// the regions have very little. Economy: pharmaceuticals (Krka in
// Novo mesto, Lek/Sandoz), the Renault/Revoz plant at Novo mesto,
// the Port of Koper as landlocked Central Europe's Adriatic
// gateway, Gorenje in Velenje, tourism at Bled and the coast, and
// a strong tech and gaming sector in Ljubljana.
// TIMEZONE: CET (UTC+1) / CEST (UTC+2 summer) — 1-2 hours behind
// Nairobi EAT, the standard European framing.
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

