  faqs: [
    { q: 'Is homeschooling legal in Slovenia?', a: 'Yes, and unusually for the region the Basic School Act frames it as a right rather than a permission. Parents notify the school where the child is enrolled in writing, by mid-August under the amended Act and for each school year separately, without giving reasons — and the school issues no permission or decision.' },
    { q: 'What are the obligations after the notification?', a: 'The child must attain at least an educational standard equivalent to the compulsory public-school programme, and their knowledge is assessed at the end of every school year by the enrolling school, in Slovene, against the national curriculum — with the subjects assessed widened by the reform equalising home-educated pupils with school pupils. Home-educated pupils also sit the national assessment (NPZ).' },
    { q: 'What happens if our child does not pass?', a: 'A pupil in grades 1-8 who does not succeed at the re-assessment must continue their basic education in a public or private school the following year; a grade 9 pupil may repeat the year or sit a resit. It is the strictest element of the framework and the reason we plan the assessment calendar first.' },
    { q: 'Can Smartious carry the assessment for us?', a: 'No. It is conducted by the school where your child is enrolled and it is your obligation with them. We provide the live teaching, the documentation, Slovene-language preparation planned into the year, and the external Cambridge examinations alongside.' },
    { q: 'Can we start home education mid-year?', a: 'No — the right is exercised for a school year, with notification by mid-August. The amended Act does allow interrupting home education during the year to return to school. Confirm current dates with your school, since this detail moved recently.' },
    { q: 'Is it true the framework ends at fifteen?', a: 'Basic school is nine years, ending around fifteen, and upper secondary is not compulsory — so the A-Level years fall outside the notification, the equivalent-standard requirement, and the year-end assessment entirely.' },
    { q: 'How does Smartious compare with the Ljubljana international schools?', a: 'They are established campuses with in-person culture and a local peer group we cannot replicate, and the public international department at Danila Kumar is a genuinely good and low-cost answer where a place exists. Smartious runs live small-group teaching at USD 2,160-6,480 a year — the supplement, the bridge, and the only complete option outside the capital.' },
    { q: 'Where do Slovenian students sit Cambridge examinations?', a: 'At authorised provision confirmed per family per session — Ljubljana is checked first, with Austrian, Italian, and Croatian centres practical for border families given the country\'s size.' },
    { q: 'Can my child access Slovenian universities with A-Levels or an IB Diploma?', a: 'Slovenian universities admit foreign secondary qualifications through recognition procedures, with programme-specific requirements, entrance examinations in some fields, and Slovene-language proficiency for Slovene-taught degrees — confirmed per programme. Austrian, Italian, Croatian, and German universities are all within a few hours and assess both routes routinely, and UCAS and the Common Application stand open internationally.' },
    { q: 'Which parts of Slovenia does Smartious cover?', a: 'Ljubljana, Maribor, Koper and the coast, Kranj and the Julian Alps, Novo mesto, and Nova Gorica have dedicated pages with local context. Live online delivery works identically anywhere in the country — which outside the capital is precisely the point.' },
  ],

  ctaH: 'Book an assessment for your child',
